<h2>The sequence of clocks</h2>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Reporting</h3><p>Section 19 POCSO obliges anyone with knowledge of an offence to report it; the police record the information and, where the child needs care, arrange protection within statutory timeframes under the Act and Rules.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Investigation — two months</h3><p>Section 193(2) BNSS mandates completion of investigation within two months of recording the information for offences under Sections 64 to 71 BNS and Sections 4, 6, 8 and 10 of the POCSO Act.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Child's evidence — thirty days</h3><p>Section 35(1) POCSO: the Special Court records the child's evidence within thirty days of cognizance, with reasons for any delay placed on record.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>Trial — one year</h3><p>Section 35(2) POCSO: the trial is to be completed, as far as possible, within one year of cognizance.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>What each clock does — and does not do</h2>

<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Clock</th><th>Source</th><th>Consequence of breach</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Two-month investigation</td><td>Section 193(2) BNSS</td><td>No automatic lapse; grounds expedition requests and judicial monitoring of investigative delay</td></tr>
  <tr><td>60/90-day chargesheet</td><td>Section 187(3) BNSS</td><td>Indefeasible right to default bail if availed before the chargesheet is filed</td></tr>
  <tr><td>30-day evidence of the child</td><td>Section 35(1) POCSO</td><td>Delay must be explained on record; supports early-listing applications</td></tr>
  <tr><td>One-year trial</td><td>Section 35(2) POCSO</td><td>Statutory target; prolonged breach weighs in bail and expedition applications</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The distinction between the two-month investigation mandate and the default-bail clock deserves emphasis, because the two are routinely conflated. The first is addressed to the investigating agency and the supervising court; it disciplines the investigation but creates no right of release. The second is addressed to the liberty of the accused: once the 90th (or 60th) day of custody passes without a chargesheet, the right to default bail crystallises on application, and it is extinguished only if the chargesheet arrives before the right is exercised.</p>

<h2>Why the timelines matter to each participant</h2>

<p>For the child and the supporting family, the thirty-day evidence rule is the provision to insist upon. The single most protective feature of a POCSO trial is early, well-prepared recording of the child's testimony in the child-friendly setting the Act mandates — screens or vulnerable witness court rooms, a support person under the 2020 Rules, and questioning routed through the court under Section 33. Once that evidence is on record, the pressure that delay places on a child witness largely dissipates.</p>

<p>For the defence, the clocks are a map of legitimate remedies: scrutiny of what the investigating officer did within the first two months, a default bail application timed to Section 187(3), and — where an incarcerated accused faces a trial drifting well past the one-year target without fault on his side — a bail application grounded in the jurisprudence on delayed trials. None of these remedies requires, or excuses, obstruction of the trial itself; adjournments sought by the defence are the first thing a court examines when a delay-based plea is made.</p>

<p>For investigating officers and prosecutors, the timelines are a checklist against which supervisory courts increasingly measure performance: forensic samples dispatched promptly, the Section 183 BNSS statement of the child recorded early, the medical examination under Section 27 POCSO completed, and the chargesheet positioned well inside the default-bail horizon.</p>

<div class="note"><p>This is a general explainer on statutory timelines in POCSO matters under the law in force since 1 July 2024, with CrPC equivalents noted where useful. It is not legal advice, and individual cases turn on their own facts and record.</p></div>
